=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1
diagnosis_text:
The border router r1 has lost its OSPF process entirely from its running configuration. The stored `/etc/frr/frr.conf` on r1 contains a well-formed OSPF block: router-id 10.0.0.76, network statements for the two core links (10.0.0.32/30 to csw1 and 10.0.0.36/30 to csw2), and `default-information originate` — the command that injects r1's kernel default route (learned via DHCP from ISPA at 203.0.113.1) into the OSPF domain. However, r1's live running-config contains only a loopback and no `router ospf` stanza. As a direct consequence, r1 forms no OSPF adjacency with csw1 or csw2 (confirmed: r1's router-id 10.0.0.76 is absent from csw1's neighbor table), so the default route is never redistributed into the domain. csw1, csw2, dswa1, dswa2 — every distribution and core switch — holds no default route in either its FRR table or the Linux kernel. When PC1 sends traffic toward Internet addresses, dswa1 (the VLAN 10 gateway) performs a kernel routing lookup, finds no match, and returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" to PC1; TCP applications experience this as a hang because they typically ignore host-unreachable ICMP errors and keep retrying until the SYN timer fires. The separate observation of TCP to Office B (10.3.0.0/24) also timing out is due to a pre-existing standing iptables FORWARD DROP rule on dswa1 (lines 62–63 of dswa1's startup, a known baseline inter-office security policy, not an injected fault). The root cause is: r1's FRR OSPF process is missing from the running configuration while it is present in `/etc/frr/frr.conf`. Corrective fix: on r1, reload the stored configuration (`vtysh -f /etc/frr/frr.conf` or `service frr reload`), which will restart the OSPF process, form adjacencies with csw1 and csw2, and begin advertising the default route to the entire OSPF domain, restoring Internet connectivity for all internal hosts.
=== END ===